# Line-ending and diff policy.
#
# Git stores LF in the object database. The working tree is normalised to LF on
# every platform so that a build produced on Windows is byte-identical to one
# produced on Linux CI. Reproducibility across machines is a DOD-BUILD
# requirement ("no developer-machine-specific assumptions").

* text=auto eol=lf

# POSIX interpreters read the shebang literally. A CRLF `gradlew` fails on Linux
# with "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" — a confusing failure that
# only appears in CI, never on the developer's machine. Pin it explicitly.
*.sh        text eol=lf
gradlew     text eol=lf

# Windows batch interpreters require CRLF.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# Never normalise binaries. gradle-wrapper.jar is committed by design: it is the
# mechanism by which a clean clone can build without a preinstalled Gradle.
*.jar       binary
*.zip       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gif       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.keystore  binary
*.p12       binary

# Generated files: exclude from language statistics and collapse in diffs.
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar linguist-vendored
